@inherits LayoutComponentBase

<header class="site-header">
    <NavMenu />
</header>

<main>
    <article class="content ft-tool-content">
        @Body
    </article>
</main>

@*
    F-01, tool half. The footer is a SIBLING of <main>, not a child of it. A <footer> element
    exposes the contentinfo landmark only at the top level of the document; nested inside
    main/article/section/aside/nav it is generic, which is where the landing page's footer
    used to sit and is the whole of what F-01 was. Enclosing <div>s (index.html's #app) do not
    count -- only those five elements do.

    The line itself is the point, not the landmark. The privacy promise lived only on the
    landing page, so the one page where a user actually types their finances never made it,
    and anyone who bookmarks or deep-links /find-my-footing/ never saw it at all. The landmark
    is the free side effect.

    Moving the footer out of main costs it main's width box, so .ft-tool-footer reproduces
    that box itself and .ft-tool-content hands over .content's bottom padding -- see app.css.
    Footing.Site/index.html carries the identical arrangement for the landing page.
*@
<footer class="ft-tool-footer">
    <p class="ft-privacy-note">Everything here stays in your browser.</p>
</footer>
